Baseline build is fine. Now let's write the treefiles.c implementation. Based on upstream nbd-3.15/3.17 (GPL, same project — this is restoring the feature), adapted to fit this tree:

- Use `lseek` instead of `myseek` (which doesn't exist in this tree).
- Include set: lfs.h first (as upstream did), then system headers, glib, then project headers. In this tree, nbdsrv.h itself includes lfs.h first; but following upstream, include lfs.h explicitly at the top.

One important detail: config.h must be included before lfs.h (lfs.h itself includes config.h — good, it has `#include "config.h"` inside). lfs.h defines _FILE_OFFSET_BITS if NBD_LFS. For this to be effective, it must be included before any system headers that use off_t. nbdsrv.c's pattern: config.h, nbd-debug.h, nbdsrv.h (which includes lfs.h) — wait, does nbd-debug.h include config.h? Yes, nbd-debug.h includes config.h. OK.

For treefiles.c, mirror the upstream ordering:

```c
#include "lfs.h"

#include <fcntl.h>
#include <inttypes.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <sys/types.h>

#include <glib.h>

#include <cliserv.h>
#include <nbd-debug.h>
#include <nbdsrv.h>
#include "treefiles.h"
```

Hmm — upstream also included backend.h and config.h. backend.h isn't needed (no punch_hole here). config.h is included via lfs.h. errno.h is needed for errno/EEXIST/ENOENT — upstream didn't include errno.h?! It uses errno, EEXIST, ENOENT… probably pulled in indirectly via glib. Let me include <errno.h> explicitly — clean.

Do we need stdlib.h? For mkstemp — mkstemp is declared in stdlib.h. Upstream didn't include stdlib.h… glib probably pulls it in. Include <stdlib.h> to be safe.

Next, functions. Let me write them faithfully to upstream with minor cleanups:

```c
/**
 * Tree structure helper functions
 */
void construct_path(char* name, int lenmax, off_t size, off_t pos, off_t* ppos) {
	if (lenmax<10)
		err("Char buffer overflow. This is likely a bug.");

	if (size<TREEDIRSIZE*TREEPAGESIZE) {
		// we are done, add filename
		snprintf(name,lenmax,"/FILE%04" PRIX64,(uint64_t)(pos/TREEPAGESIZE) % TREEDIRSIZE);
		*ppos = pos / (TREEPAGESIZE*TREEDIRSIZE);
	} else {
		construct_path(name+9,lenmax-9,size/TREEDIRSIZE,pos,ppos);
		char buffer[10];
		snprintf(buffer,sizeof(buffer),"/TREE%04" PRIX64,(uint64_t)(*ppos % TREEDIRSIZE));
		memcpy(name,buffer,9); // copy into string without trailing zero
		*ppos/=TREEDIRSIZE;
	}
}
```

Wait: `(pos/TREEPAGESIZE) % TREEDIRSIZE` — precedence: cast binds tighter than %, so `(uint64_t)(pos/TREEPAGESIZE) % TREEDIRSIZE` means ((uint64_t)(pos/TREEPAGESIZE)) % TREEDIRSIZE. Good.

`err()` — from cliserv.h: `#define err(S) nbd_err(S)`. nbd_err does exit(EXIT_FAILURE). In threaded server context, exit inside worker thread terminates whole server. That's upstream behavior; preserve (only triggered on truly broken config, e.g. buffer overflow or failed open of created file).

Hmm, wait — one important consideration about `err("Error opening tree block file %m")`: err_nonfatal handles %m substitution. Good.

```c
void delete_treefile(char* name, off_t size, off_t pos) {
	char filename[256+strlen(name)];
	strcpy(filename,name);
	off_t ppos;
	construct_path(filename+strlen(name),256,size,pos,&ppos);

	DEBUG("Deleting treefile: %s",filename);

	if (unlink(filename)==-1)
		DEBUG("Deleting failed : %s",strerror(errno));
}
```

VLA `char filename[256+strlen(name)]` — no problem (project uses C with VLAs elsewhere? e.g. `char privmask[strlen(mask)+1]` in nbdsrv.c — yes, VLA used).

```c
